<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keladi Chennamma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1696">1696</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> Chennamma was queen of the Keladi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> kingdom in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, ruling from 1671 until her death in 1696 after the death of her husband Somashekhara Nayaka. She defended the kingdom against neighbouring powers and, around 1689, gave refuge to Rajaram, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, as he fled the Mughal army towards Jinji, enduring a Mughal attack in consequence. She is remembered as one of the notable women rulers of Karnataka.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keladi-chennamma.jpg" alt="Keladi Chennamma" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balraj D · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganapathi_%26_Keladi_Chennamma_Statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chennamma was born in the household of Siddappa Shetty, a merchant of the Kundapur region, and married King Somashekara Nayaka in 1667. After his death she took charge of the administration of the Keladi Nayaka kingdom, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Ikkeri Nayakas</a>, and governed it for 25 years from Keladi near Sagara. She adopted one of her close relatives, Basavappa Nayaka, who succeeded her as Hiriya Basappa Nayaka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Her reign is regarded as a period of administrative consolidation and economic stability. She kept the Shist land revenue system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivappa-nayaka">Shivappa Nayaka</a> but gave tax holidays to farmers who cleared new forest land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a>. The state kept its monopoly on black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, the main export, and by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barkur">Barkur</a> with the Portuguese in 1678 she kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the coastal ports while collecting fixed customs duties. A devout Veerashaiva, she granted the village of Kuduvalli tax-free to the Sringeri Sharada Peetham in 1691 and let the Portuguese build churches at Mirjan, Honnavara, Chandavara and Kalyanpura.</p><p>When Chikka Devaraja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> took several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> territories, among them Magadi, Kadur and Channarayadurga, the queen, as regent for her adopted son Basava Raja, defeated the Mysore army at Vasudhare and recovered Kadur, Banavara, Hassan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>. Expecting Mughal attack, she moved the treasury and records to the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bidanur</a> (Nagara) and strengthened it with stone-lined water channels and granaries for long sieges.</p><p>In late 1689 Chhatrapati Rajaram I, fleeing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s execution of his half-brother Sambhaji, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bidanur</a> pursued by a Mughal army under Kasim Khan. Chennamma gave him supplies and an escort through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> forests. Aurangzeb then sent Kasim Khan to besiege Bidanur, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>-drenched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> her Bedar infantry raided the Mughal supply lines, and after heavy losses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> withdrew. A treaty of 1690 recognised the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Channagiri is named after her. She is counted, with Rani Chennabhairadevi, Abbakka Rani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittur-chennamma">Kittur Chennamma</a>, Belawadi Mallamma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>, among the foremost women warriors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, and her life is the subject of Keladi Chennamma, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> historical series on Suvarna TV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keladi Chennamma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Keladi Chennamma</b>, who ruled from 1671, sheltered Shivaji's son Rajaram from the Mughal Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
